 paste, cut and paste...","caption":"Photoshop Phun","poi_name":"Lower Mesa Falls","subject":"Jeremy Laucks","author":"Gordon Dalton, Jeremy Laucks","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/2499","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/2499"}},{"id":"2356","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"3123","description":"Top half of Lower Mesa. This 20'er leads into the 40' drop.","caption":"Lower Mesa Falls, top drop","poi_name":"Lower Mesa Falls","subject":"Gordon Dalton","author":"Josh Sandage","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/2356","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/2356"}},{"id":"2358","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"3123","description":"Hawaii? Ireland? Iceland? No - it's the Potato State! This is the happy, misty pool below the falls. ","caption":"Below the falls","poi_name":"Lower Mesa Falls","subject":"Josh Sandage","author":"Gordon Dalton","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/2358","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/2358"}},{"id":"2361","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"3123","description":"A shot of the hike-out which follows a huck off Lower Mesa. It rocks.","caption":"After the huck.","poi_name":"","subject":"Post-huck hike.","author":"Gordon Dalton","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/2361","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/2361"}},{"id":"2534","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"3124","description":"The second tier of Lower Mesa Falls.","caption":"Lower Mesa Falls","poi_name":"Lower Mesa Falls, 2nd drop","subject":"Gordon Dalton","author":"J. Laucks","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/2534","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/2534"}},{"id":"2016","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"4577","description":"Not happy after digging the car out of the snow all night, and then at the start of the 3mile trek to the falls in the snow.","caption":"TRAILHEAD to the FALLS","poi_name":"Lower Mesa Falls","subject":"Cody Howard","author":"Spencer Unger","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/2016","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/2016"}},{"id":"40416","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"8843","description":"Joe Booth runs the lower left teir of Lower Mesa Falls.  Pat Rogers took this picture from the river right platue","caption":"Lower Teir, Left Side","poi_name":"Lower Mesa Falls","subject":"Joe Booth","author":"Pat Rogers","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/40416","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/40416"}}],"detail":{"id":"559","permitUrl":null,"permitInfo":null,"description":"TO GET THERE: Mesa Falls Senic Drive near Ashton/Island Park. There is a road that comes out of the Upper Mesa Falls parking lot that leads to the clearing next to the Lower Mesa Falls.\n\nTHE RUN: Lower Mesa Falls is great park and huck spot. The usual line is the river left side. It is like a 20 foot fall into a pool that leads into a 45 footer. The sign says 65 foot total drop, the first is definitely around 20 foot, and the second drop is about 30-35'.\n\nIt's pretty sic!\n\nThe right side has been run, you can see the descents of the right in the opening scene of Valahalla, and No Big Names. MAKE sure that you bring your camera!\n\nBelow Mesa Falls the river is mild and easy.\n\nReference:\n\nGrant Amaral's book, Idaho: the Whitewater State (Watershed, 1992).\n\nLatitude / Longitude data are approximate.\n\nAlternate takeout: US 191-20 near Ashton (9 mi. dow